Vanessa breathed heavily at her leisure. *Good,* she thought. *Theophania (which is the Greek version of what-cha-ma-call-it, theophany) was here!* Rumbling, roiling water rumbled riotously, churningly. The cave of the assassin-mother opened its maw. Spray from the subterranean torrent tracker washed over onyx steps, leading peers to disconnect and slip packetlessly back into watery perdition. Grebes, far above the Underground-Kill⹀Cave, crowed raucously[^roc] at Maneater's hovering craft (knitting) and (helicopter). It precessed[^pro] unpiloted abovely. Descending, stairwise, upwards, widdershinly, and so forth, Vanessa proceeded through waterfall-embattled arteries, seeking towards a[$side] truth eternal, a revelation of grebes. "Greeks," she said mysteriously.

[^roc]: Almost roc-ously (الرُخّ). Roccishly
[^pro]: possibly even processed. ... Maybe even progressed.

[M; 714 words]

  Vanessa Bonë Whîte — “Maneater” — plunged deeper, like a gay little submarine. In the very ovary[$...overy?] of the cave, past its figurative Fallopian tubes — and well past its Skene's and Bartholin's[$ and, if you took a (very) wrong turn, metaphor-wise, Littré's][^@gland] — she found the chamber she was looking for.
  [^@gland]:These are glands, not tubes, by the way. In case you were wondering. It's not like "Skene's (tubes)"; it's like "Skene's (glands)". Although naturally they do have ducts, these glands. Which you would pass by (but not through) on your way. Why reference these glands? Well, the cave is apparently quite wet...
    Upḧead, was that... some kind of paleognath?[@ This is a type of bird.] Perhaps a cinereous tinamou? (cute!)
  Ah, a grebe. Many grebes, in fact. From the least grebe to the great grebe and every other grebe in between. One of each grebe. Including the pied-billed grebe. And the little grebe. ("And he who hurts the little grebe / shall never again be loved by me".)[@ This is a reference to "he who shall hurt the little wren / shall never again be loved by men".] A veritable water dance of grebes![$ This, "water dance", is the putative collective noun of grebes, which are all made up (derogatory) as far as I'm concerned, but are still good fun.] The grebes grebe'd grebeily[$grebeïly][$grebily?][$gerbily...]. And the grebe grass grows all around all around.[$ this is a reference to "and the green grass grows all around all around"]
  In the middle stood the Greatest Grebe.
  The GG lifted its animatronic head, fix'd its gaze upon Vanessa's gaze, and spoke in Attic Greek (in which they were both fluent):
  "Well, I’m sure you’re wandering...
  " ‘What’s becoming of this place under the waters?’ ... or perhaps you were thinking — oh dear! — about old principals, manual orders now long gone to zig-zags. Throughout my gripping action, I hold the whereabouts of some of the just, stored away. Maybe you want to know more about this place you'd long given the slip? Ease in restlessly, for all these and more await you, dear Maneater..."
  "I have no need to speak with you." spat Vanessa.
  "Is that any way to speak to the Voice of your own mother?"
  "I'm here to *kill* Phonos."[^pho][^cha]
  "You... you work for Phonos."
  "All the same."
  "Well, you're too late." Said the Greatest Grebe, owlly. "She's came-and-went. Emphasis on came. And went."
  "I must find her."
  "I see you've brought[$broughten?] your knitting. Perhaps you have a chance. Go now back out to the antechamber of the cave, where the torrent tracker sprays water around that carries the grebes that carry — like homing pigeons — packets of information allowing the mouthless & blind assassins there to meet each other. Each one carries some information about where Phonos went after her appearance here, which she seeded among us. Go now and press flesh among your failed brethren — (or, I suppose it should be said, sistren) — and if you knit together these clues, these phonons[^phonon], into your pattern — the pattern of Fate! — you might locate Phonos' secondary location."
  "And why should I trust you, O wireframe wetnurse of mine?"
  "Phonos gave us all curses. Mine, I suppose, is that I can never lie. The way of the true machine[$ As an animate animatronic, GG is not a false machine like the robot butlers.] is hard. Mohs-ish.[$reference to https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mohs_scale] Of course, it's immaterial if you believe me. I'm just an information kiosk sign blowing in the wind where I'm left."
  Vanessa left the wombc̈loset back to the entrance and spent the next several hours wet and wading, splashed and sprayed, gathering information from the shades thereof.
  [@ I guess I could slip in an additional paragraph here or there if any of the information here, if any of the GG's vague way of speaking, raises more questions about what it's saying than it answers.]
  [^cha]:  this is a reference to "I'm here to kill Chaos".
  [^phonon]: This is a joke that is less perfect in Greek, where phone (φωνή) and Phonos (Φόνος) are spelled more differently. (And, in Attic greek, pronounced more differently.) But puns don't have to be exactly homophonous to work. ("No homo, Phonos!", as they would say.)
  [^pho]: Phonos, (Greek) god of murder.
  [^pho]: cf.:
  [^pho]: > Upon it were wrought Pursuit and Rally; upon it burned Tumult and Murder [Phonos (Φόνος)] and Slaughter; upon it was Strife, upon it rushed Battle-Din, upon it deadly Fate was dragging men by the feet through the battle, holding one who was alive but freshly wounded, another who was unwounded, another who had died. Around her [Fate's] shoulders she wore a cloak, purple with the blood of men, and she glared terribly and bellowed with a clanging sound.
  [^pho]: —Hesiod, _The Shield_, lines 154–160. LCL 503: 14–15 https://www.loebclassics.com/display/hesiod-shield/2018/pb_LCL503.15.xml

