Burger Guthrie found Thomas standing in the street, looking blank and at nothing. "All good, boss?"

Thomas spoke the way you hold a wounded bird in two cupped hands. "I think someone I dearly loved may have died.[@ I guess Vanessa can't sense people in Heaven. Or maybe they just count as infertile in whatever way matters? ~C] He was my cousin, but I only really knew him for about half an hour a couple days ago."

Bus was silent a moment. "I'm sorry, Thomas."

The intuitive detective straightened. "It's also possible that he's just infertile. Which wouldn't be a terrible problem for him, so far as I know."

Bus was silent a moment. "What?"

"Tell you later. My God, Bus, are you okay?"

"I've told you before, I'm not God, I just have good hair.[@ I'm less pleased with this than when I first wrote it, but I'm leaving it in out of inertia/trusting in past me's brilliance. ~C] Nicked me several times," Grover Beveland said with a shrug. The blood on his shirt made it move oddly with the gesture. "I've had worse, as you very well know. Remember the incident in the food truck with that pelican?"

Thomas shuddered. "_No_, if I can help it. That was horrible; I thought I was gonna lose you."

"Aw, c'mon boss, you know I'm in it for the long haul."[$ The long hall?! Has Gurton been in the Backrooms?] He punched Thomas on the arm, then looked around. "I see that you haven't been perforated, but I also don't see an arrested perforateer. What happened?"

"She left. In a helicopter, I couldn't nab her. She got spooked by missing you repeatedly and my running towards her. By the way, I don't think nicks count as perforation, topologically speaking, so I think it should be _attempted_ perforateer."

Groucho Barks[$ New dog name unlocked.] shook his head. "The man in the room across the hall was shot in the gut. What's our next move?"

Thomas clenched his teeth. He was desperate to resume his quest to find, and save if necessary/possible, Paul and Prudence, but it wouldn't be right to rush off and leave a wounded innocent behind. And the Track... "How bad was the wound? We'll do what we can for him and grab the photos."

Gust pulled the folder from his jacket's[@ I can't remember if any details of Gus' wardrobe have been established by this point and I can't be bothered to look. ~C][@ I'm just a sucker for pulling folders out of outerwear. ~C] inner pocket and waved them. "I'm no chiropractor, but a bunch of the neighbors were on the phone with emergency services when I left. They'll be here soon."

Thomas closed his eyes.

---

By this point, it has been established that the citizens of Skoios are an idiosyncratic lot. They have strange names. They have strange names for the objects they surround themselves with. Perhaps they are a little spoiled by the rarefied air they breathe, possibly a little dismissive of Continental affairs.

But it has also been established that they enjoy the finer things. Really enjoy _them_, not being seen to have them by themselves or others. To thrust their faces into the piquancy, to push buttons, to plaster makeup on faces and advertisements on the blades of stealth helicopters, to dabble in recursive beverages and the eggs of resurrected animals. Even their well-to-do, who in other climes are the most listless of men, have a zest for their possessions, activities, and sensations. Dimly aware of what's expected of them as inheritors of the future, with charming pageantry they encase their servants in suits of rigid chrome to ape the servitors their ancestors dreamed of.

In the background of every scene on the island, rising above the skyline in sensual curves _sans_ the rigid grammar of anatomy, are the false promises of danger. The Jezebels. The Digsxby rollercoasters[$ rollers-coaster]. The Skoions do not mislike the tang of artifice. And as in the danger proffered (with a wink) by the rollercoasters there is a hint of truth, so there is something blatant, shameless, honest, open, in the artificiality of the flying islanders. In their gaudy makeup and gauche make-believe.[^Michaelmas]

Their feet they kept firmly planted on the earth, for they learned that practicality, artifice, and technique were the best way to get what they wanted. And, looking up, they wanted to get their heads into the clouds. The result was predictable, really: the earth rose under their feet.

And thus it was, when the islands first took to the skies and the society upon them was being builded,[$dialectical past tense], and the councils met to bid the New Alchemists with what substance to pave their highways and the Alchemists promised everlasting hardness[$ More than 4 hours, yes.] and impermeability and smoothness, that an old man stood and said for all the people, "It must have the smell. The smell in the summers of my boyhood. Whatever you must put in it, whatever charms you must utter or reagents you must autoclave, it must have that smell after rain."

[^Michaelmas]: It was this that Michaelmas Term, sharing the general Skoion love for quiddity, could not share. Like God, he could love what was really there; not what all begged him, with tears in their eyes, to pretend was there.

---

It had rained in the night.

Thomas stood in the street and breathed in the smell of the pavement and the damp leaves[$ the wet leaves don't get 5 paragraphs of justification.] and the gun[$in play]smoke. The drips and the small dopplering wail of approaching sirens. Glad warmth from above and soft chill from below washing over his skin like ripples meeting in a pond.

And beneath the skin?

It was part of his system, like his nerves, his bones. His recent doubts changed nothing fundamental. However it had arisen, now it was him. An integral piece.

Thomas opened his eyes. "It's time to hotwire a car."[$ Skoion road-cars are manufactured so that they can be hotwired.]

---
Bert Graccus slid the car's door open; he had picked the one vehicle in the street which the owner had left un-biolocked.[$ Yes. Consumer cars have biometric scanners but are manufactured to be hotwired.] He stepped back and Thomas crouched and fiddled.[$ On several of their prior adventurers[$ See _The Tale of the Incident in the Food Truck with That Pelican_] Thomas had hotwired a car via the guidance of the Track. Keen to learn, he had paid close attention to what he did, and could now perform the feat with only the occasional psuedo-oracular nudge.]

As Thomas crouched and fiddled, Gurgle said, "Since we used our real names checking into the motel, the police will know we were there..."

Pause. Resume. "Well, good. Maybe it'll galvanize them. Maybe if they try to track us down, they'll catch up at some crucial juncture and actually be of some use." Thomas sighed. "I just hope they don't jump to some wrong but reasonable conclusion and try to arrest us."

Bog-Gob directed a somewhat pitying look at the back of his partner's head. "You know they will."

After a couple of minutes, Thomas stepped back in his turn and Goblintz got in. Thomas had always been curiously incapable behind the wheel. He got in on the other side.

"Where to, boss?"

With breath that was not bated but would have been bated would bating it not defeat the point of the exercise, Thomas said:

"1440 Ratcliffe Court."[@ This is where Vanessa said she last felt Paul Eeny. ~C]

Had his breath been bated, he would have released it in surprised relief, but it wasn't so he couldn't, but still.

Glovebox reached across to the glovebox and withdrew a map, which he studied. "Says it's the Chapel of Good Thule of Rumb. What religion is that? Oh, it's right next to the First Baptist Museum of the Underworld."

"Ah." Thomas reached over in his turn and took the folder of photos from Bus's coat. "Let's have a closer look at these..."

They pulled away from the curb. "Like, right next to it. The chapel seems to be inside the parking lot of the museum. Like, if we went there and happened to—through some outrageous coincidences—just miss a couple of other people who were also going there, it might really annoy any hypothetical people who are following our activities."

"I see."

They nearly ran over Regular Belasco.

Thomas lowered the window. "Where do you think you're going?"

"I—I don't know."

"Great! Get in, we'll take you there."

